// Bank — dynamic Namespace-backed lockbox custody service.
//
// Bank is a dynamic CLOOMC LUMP at catalog index 54, not a hardware boot
// slot. The inner sanctum creates private Outform storage only when needed.
//
// Security ABI:
//   * every public authority is a typed capability register;
//   * offsets, word counts, kinds, capacities, and status values remain DR
//     values; and
//   * the live binding keeps the inner-sanctum passkey private and manages
//     custody, zeroization, and recovery.
//
// The source models lifecycle state only. It cannot manufacture, inspect, or
// reconstruct authority from integer fields: the CR ABI and proof-bound runtime
// gate are the sole security boundary for those operations.
//
// MintKey is deliberately not a public method. The live Bank binding invokes
// it only inside the inner sanctum, where it mints or rotates the private
// passkey and retains its independent proof. No owner key, proof, scalar ID,
// Namespace address, or reconstructed handle crosses the Bank boundary.
// Callers use the higher-level methods below with typed Golden Tokens only.

abstraction Bank {
    capabilities {
    }

    // Create(lump: CR1 Inform R) -> CR0 = BankVariable Enter capability or NULL,
    // and DR0 = 1 on success or a Bank error code on failure. CR0 is the
    // resulting Enter authority; DR0 is diagnostic data and can never authorize
    // entry. The caller must test CR0 before using the verified abstraction.
    //
    // The executable policy is deliberately staged as Bank T3 approval:
    //   T3.1 recompute the canonical content address from submitted bytes;
    //   T3.2 compare every requested identity assertion mechanically;
    //   T3.3 record deferred genesis authority as human-IDE approval only;
    //   T3.4 require compiler-owned SELF == E(dot name, issue), then
    //   validate the complete submitted LUMP and fail with NULL CR0;
    //   2. commit private custody atomically (allocation, private Namespace
    //      record, proof-bound object, and copied bytes), cleaning up on error;
    //   3. issue the nullable typed BankVariable E capability only after commit.
    // The proof-bound runtime is the only layer allowed to perform those
    // private operations or retain credentials. The source expresses the
    // control-flow contract without exposing their scalar results.
    method Create(lump: capability Inform R) {
        tperm(CR1, R)
        // The native binding returns only after T3.1, T3.2, T3.3, and T3.4
        // succeed, then atomically commits private custody. It clears CR0 on
        // every approval or commit error.
        //
        // This is deliberately a capability-presence check, not a DR0 status
        // check: DR0 is diagnostic data and must never stand in for authority.
        if (CR0 != null) {
            // A non-NULL CR0 is the proof-bound BankVariable E capability issued
            // after the successful private commit.
            return(1)
        }
        // NULL CR0 means no BankVariable was issued.
        return(0)
    }

    // Read and Inspect operate on the typed E capability returned by Create in
    // CR0. Methods mediate every read of a Bank-owned abstraction; callers
    // cannot substitute another CR, a raw GT, DR0, or metadata for that capability.
    // Failed calls preserve the NULL/invalid CR0 state and identify the reason
    // in DR0 rather than collapsing all failures into one false value.
    // The binding returns a fresh readable Inform capability in CR4 for Read.
    // Inspect writes status=DR0, words=DR1, capacity=DR2, issue=DR3, and
    // lifecycle=DR4; private locations and tokens are never materialised.
    method Read(variable: capability BankVariable E, offset, words) {
        if (offset < 0) {
            return(0)
        }
        if (words <= 0) {
            return(0)
        }
        return(1)
    }

    method InspectVariable(variable: capability BankVariable E) {
        return(1)
    }

    // Release and RevokeVariable retire the verified variable, zeroize its
    // private storage, and clear CR0. A BankVariable capability (CR0) is the
    // only public authority for the verified variable it names. The binding
    // resolves that typed Golden Token to private custody state and retains
    // the passkey/proof internally; callers never receive the private record.
    // Nested variables are created by calling Create on a readable result from
    // Read, never by exposing inner-sanctum state.
    method Release(variable: capability BankVariable E) {
        return(1)
    }

    method RevokeVariable(variable: capability BankVariable E) {
        return(1)
    }

}